    "q-140493": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140493",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 4\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=4$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =4+(-2)\\text{i}=4-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} \\\\ -4+(-1)\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} \\\\ -4-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =4∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -4∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
