      "source_example_id": "e-5530",
      "knowledge_point": "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition",
      "kp_id": "e-5530"
    },
    "q-38571": {
      "dom_id": "step-q38571",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -6 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7-6\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -6 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
