      "source_example_id": "e-5532",
      "knowledge_point": "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Only One Eigenvalue",
      "kp_id": "e-5532"
    },
    "q-38570": {
      "dom_id": "step-q38570",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A$ has the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5+3\\text{i},$ and that $A=PC{P}^{-1}$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nSince we are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5+3\\text{i},$ we can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-5+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-5+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3-3\\text{i} & -3 \\\\ 6 & -3-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(3-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+(-3-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(1-\\text{i}){x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (1-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
